desc_intent:
Specify a model of a bounded symbolic model-checking procedure that explores all executions of a transition system up to a fixed maximum length. The model should treat the transition relation as partitioned into a finite set of named transitions and, at every depth k (1..MAX_DEPTH), record which transitions are considered for extension of the symbolic frontier. The checker must repeatedly query an SMT-like oracle about satisfiability of the constraints induced by the current frontier: the oracle responds "sat" if it can witness a concrete execution (sequence of transitions) in the set represented by the frontier that violates a given safety property, and "unsat" otherwise.

The model should support:
- initialization that encodes initial transitions into the frontier and immediately detects an initial invariant violation if present;
- an exploration step that (nondeterministically) selects any subset of transitions to attempt to extend the frontier at the current depth, asks the oracle whether any extension yields a violation, and either reports a counterexample and halts in an error state, or updates the frontier and increments depth;
- detection of deadlock when no transitions are selectable for extension at a depth;
- termination when a counterexample is found, when a deadlock occurs, or when the maximum depth is reached without finding a violation.

Specify key correctness properties to be checked:
- safety preservation: the checker should not reach an error state unless the oracle legitimately reports a violating execution;
- completeness up to the bound: if there exists any concrete violating execution whose length is ≤ MAX_DEPTH, the model must allow the checker to reach the error state;
- proper termination: the checker eventually stops in one of the terminal states (found error, no-error at bound, or deadlock).

Specify a model of a distributed client that interacts with servers using session-based request/response messaging. The model should describe many clients (identified by distinct client IDs) that can open and close sessions with servers and send sequenced command requests while awaiting asynchronous responses. Each client must be able to:
- initiate a session establishment request to a chosen server when it has no active session;
- close an active session by sending a session close request to the server that currently serves that session;
- send command requests to the server that holds the client’s current session, attaching a locally incrementing command sequence number with each request;
- receive and process three kinds of responses from servers: session-open confirmations that install a session identifier for the client, session-close confirmations that clear the client’s session, and command responses that acknowledge individual commands.

The model must capture an asynchronous, unreliable but fair message network where messages can be delayed and reordered but are eventually delivered unless explicitly discarded by operations that consume responses. The correctness properties to verify are:
- Safety: a client never has more than one active session identifier at a time; command requests are only sent when a client has an active session; responses are only applied to the intended recipient client.
- Session integrity: after receiving an open confirmation the client records the session id it was assigned, and after a close confirmation the client no longer believes it has a session.
- Command monotonicity: each client’s command sequence numbers strictly increase with every issued command.
- Liveness (under fair scheduling): a request that is sent and not lost will eventually lead to the corresponding response being processed by its intended client, enabling progress of session open/close and command completion.

Specify a formal model of finite zero-indexed sequences (arrays indexed 0..n-1, with a distinct empty sequence) and basic operations on them. The model should treat a sequence’s length, valid index set, and membership in the set of all sequences over a given element set as first-class concepts. Provide precise definitions for:
- the empty zero-indexed sequence and the domain of valid indices for any sequence;
- the length function that returns a natural number and differentiates the empty sequence;
- conversion functions between zero-indexed sequences and the conventional one-indexed sequence representation (including the empty case), ensuring round-trip consistency.

Include ordering and transformation operators and state their intended behavior and correctness properties:
- a lexicographic total preorder on sequences of potentially different lengths that compares elementwise and treats shorter prefixes appropriately;
- a left-rotation operator that rotates a nonempty sequence by a given offset modulo its length;
- the function that returns the set of all distinct rotations of a sequence.

Specify key invariants and correctness properties the model must guarantee:
- length and index-set definitions are consistent with one another and preserved by conversion and rotation operations;
- conversions between index conventions are inverses when applied in both directions (modulo empty sequence);
- lexicographic comparison is total and deterministic for any two sequences drawn from the same element domain;
- rotation preserves multiset of elements and length, and the rotations set contains exactly one rotation per offset (no duplicates) for nonempty sequences.

Do not constrain implementation; describe only the abstract data types, operations, and the properties they must satisfy.

Specify a model of a distributed heartbeat-based failure detection service for a fixed set of processes under partial synchrony. The model should describe each process having a local, discrete clock that advances in steps; processes periodically send heartbeat ("alive") messages to all others, periodically make failure-suspicions based on locally observed time since last message from each peer, and update per-peer timeout estimates when messages are heard. The environment may nondeterministically deliver, drop, or delay messages subject to arbitrary finite delays.

The specification must capture:
- periodic sending and periodic suspicion-checking events driven by each local clock (these periods are distinct and independent);
- for each observer-process and each target-process, a local counter measuring time since last received heartbeat and a per-observer per-target timeout value that can increase when a heartbeat is received;
- rules for receiving batches of messages in a transition that reset counters for observed senders and possibly increase their timeouts;
- a suspicion rule that marks a peer faulty whenever the local counter exceeds the currently used timeout for that peer;
- sending rule that produces heartbeat messages for all peers when a sender's clock reaches its send period.

Key correctness properties to guarantee and be checkable:
- Safety (no incorrect permanent suspicion): any correct process (one that continues to send heartbeats indefinitely) is only finitely often suspected by a correct observer; in particular, if a process continues to send heartbeats, observers eventually stop suspecting it.
- Liveness (completeness): every process that has actually crashed (stops sending) is eventually permanently suspected by every correct observer (strong completeness), or at least by some observers (weak completeness) — state which completeness goal is required.
- Convergence of estimates: for each correct observer-target pair, the per-target timeout value stabilizes to some finite value.
- Type and boundedness invariants: counters and timeouts remain integers and respect any assumed default bounds or initial values.

Do not prescribe specific variable names or implementation steps; specify only the state elements, allowable transitions, timing constraints, and the properties above.

Specify a formal model of a general-purpose model-checker runtime library that provides deterministic, observable helper operations used by a state space explorer. The model should describe services that a model checker makes available to specifications and checks, not the internals of any particular algorithm. The system must supply:
- a way to print or log values for debugging and progress reporting;
- an assertion facility that can fail a check and optionally emit diagnostic information without prescribing error-handling strategy;
- a source of nondeterministic numerical time or step counters for use in timed measurements;
- a per-worker mutable storage array with get and set operations, where each worker has an independent view after initialization, but an initial global setting can initialize all workers’ views;
- an operation that chooses an element uniformly (or nondeterministically) from any nonempty set for randomized simulation or sampling;
- operations to query lightweight runtime metrics such as number of distinct states discovered, queue length, elapsed time, current exploration depth, and maximal depth seen so far;
- utility operators for common functional manipulations: overriding maps, merging maps, producing permutations of a finite set, and stable sorting of finite sequences consistent with a provided binary comparison relation.

Require that all operations are abstract: they may be nondeterministic but must always yield values of the expected type and must not deadlock. The get/set storage semantics must ensure that sets performed before worker activation affect all workers, while later sets affect only the actor’s local view. The sampling operation must always return an element from the provided nonempty set. Specify invariants expressing these behavioral contracts and liveness assumptions sufficient for model checking client specifications that rely on these services.

Specify a formal TLA+ model of a multiset (bag) library and a small suite of unit-style properties that such a library must satisfy. The model should treat a bag as a finite map from elements to positive natural counts, and must support construction, inspection, and algebraic operations on bags without prescribing any particular implementation.

The specification must include operations to:
- construct an empty bag and a bag from a set (one copy of each element);
- test membership (an element appears at least once) and obtain the number of copies of an element;
- compute the union (add multiplicities) and difference (subtract multiplicities, floor at zero) of two bags;
- form the union of a set of bags (pointwise sum) and map a function over the elements of a bag to produce a new bag (multiplicities aggregate by image);
- enumerate or characterize all subbags of a given bag and compute the total cardinality (sum of multiplicities) of a bag;
- compute the maximum multiplicity present in a bag.

State the correctness properties these operations must satisfy for all bags over a finite universe:
- semantics: bags correspond to finite domains mapping to positive integers, membership and copy-count agree with the domain and mapping;
- algebraic laws: union is commutative and associative; empty bag is identity for union; difference followed by union yields expected inclusions; difference removes at most the available copies and yields empty when subtracting a bag from itself;
- ordering: a subbag relation compares domains and multiplicities pointwise and is preserved by union and difference (monotonicity);
- mapping and bag-union operations preserve multiplicities (mapping aggregates counts of preimages; union over a set equals repeated pairwise union);
- cardinality and maximum multiplicity computations equal the sum and max of multiplicities respectively;
- combinatorial counts: the number of subbags equals the product over elements of (1 + multiplicity), and Set-to-Bag and Bag-to-Set conversions are inverses as expected.

Also include a small set of concrete example bags and boolean assertions (unit tests) that exercise the operations and check the above properties. The model should not constrain internal representations beyond the behavioral requirements.
